  ("  barmaid" 0.1092 0.2004 0.3265 0.3427 0.3427 0.0162 nil 0.3087 0.0912)
  ("el/la cónyuge spouse" 0.1092 0.2751 0.3430 0.3594 0.3593 0.0165 0.0048 0.2665 0.0325)
  ("el/la enlace representative" 0.3853 0.5959 0.2770 0.2935 0.2934 0.0165 0.0048 0.2594 0.0325)
  ("el/la intérprete interpreter" 0.3853 0.5941 0.2935 0.3100 0.3099 0.0165 0.0048 0.2527 0.0325)
  ("el/la joven young man/" 0.3853 0.5782 0.3100 0.3265 0.3264 0.0165 0.0048 0.3557 0.0325)
  ("  young woman" 0.3853 0.5285 0.3265 0.3430 0.3427 0.0165 0.0048 0.2709 0.0740)
  ("el/la líder political leader*" 0.3853 0.5970 0.3430 0.3595 0.3594 0.0165 0.0048 0.2922 0.0325)
  ("el/la mártir martyr" 0.6614 0.8126 0.2770 0.2935 0.2934 0.0165 0.0048 0.4110 0.0325)
  ("el/la rehén hostage" 0.6614 0.8117 0.2935 0.3100 0.3099 0.0165 0.0048 0.2699 0.0325)
  ("el/la tigre (or la tigresa) tiger" 0.6614 0.8903 0.3100 0.3265 0.3264 0.0165 0.0048 0.2507 0.0325)
  ("Exceptions: el huésped/la huéspeda ‘guest’ (more usually la huésped, which the Academy recom-" 0.0920 0.9080 0.3694 0.3859 0.3857 0.0165 0.0067 0.3022 0.1000)
  ("mends), el monje/la monja ‘monk’/‘nun’, el sastre/la sastra ‘tailor’. For la jefa see 1.2.7." 0.0920 0.7969 0.3859 0.4024 0.4023 0.0165 0.0048 0.3667 0.0686)
  ("(1) *La lideresa is approved by the Academy for a female political leader, but most people say" 0.0920 0.9081 0.4146 0.4311 0.4309 0.0165 0.0067 0.2995 0.0225)
  ("la líder." 0.0920 0.1494 0.4313 0.4475 0.4475 0.0163 0.0048 0.2515 0.0139)
  ("1.2.7 Feminine forms of nouns referring to professions" 0.0920 0.7260 0.4717 0.4924 0.4924 0.0208 0.0068 0.3331 0.0541)
  ("As the social status of women improves, the stigma once attached to some feminine forms of" 0.0920 0.9081 0.5001 0.5166 0.5166 0.0165 0.0068 0.3304 0.0232)
  ("professions is vanishing. The following should be noted:" 0.0920 0.5724 0.5166 0.5331 0.5331 0.0165 0.0048 0.3140 0.0968)
  ("• El/la abogado ‘lawyer’. The form la abogada is now widely accepted, but it originally meant" 0.1092 0.9054 0.5431 0.5596 0.5594 0.0165 0.0048 0.3556 0.0627)
  ("‘intercessionary saint’." 0.1305 0.3207 0.5596 0.5761 0.5761 0.0165 0.0048 0.2602 0.1343)
  ("• La clienta ‘female customer’ is increasingly accepted, at least in Spain, but la cliente is also" 0.1092 0.8945 0.5761 0.5926 0.5924 0.0165 0.0048 0.3327 0.0438)
  ("heard." 0.1305 0.1844 0.5926 0.6091 0.6091 0.0165 nil 0.1620 0.0539)
  ("• El/la jefe: la jefa is accepted by El País as the feminine of el/la jefe ‘boss’, but it sounds too" 0.1092 0.8763 0.6091 0.6256 0.6254 0.0165 0.0048 0.3392 0.0627)
  ("familiar for some people. García Márquez (Col.) writes Maruja había sido . . . jefe de relaciones" 0.1305 0.9039 0.6256 0.6421 0.6420 0.0165 0.0048 0.3949 0.0671)
  ("públicas ‘Maruja had been head of public relations’." 0.1305 0.5634 0.6421 0.6586 0.6585 0.0165 0.0048 0.3350 0.0638)
  ("• El/la juez ‘judge’ – the preferred form in Spain, Mexico and Peru: El País insists on la juez." 0.1092 0.8902 0.6586 0.6751 0.6749 0.0165 0.0048 0.3530 0.0627)
  ("Elsewhere in Latin America la jueza is not uncommon for a female judge. The Academy" 0.1305 0.8702 0.6751 0.6916 0.6915 0.0165 0.0048 0.3882 0.0878)
  ("accepts la jueza and it is widespread in speech everywhere." 0.1305 0.6292 0.6916 0.7081 0.7080 0.0165 0.0048 0.3156 0.0620)
  ("• El médico ‘doctor’: la médica is normal in much of Latin America, cf. una médica blanca" 0.1092 0.8625 0.7081 0.7245 0.7244 0.0165 0.0050 0.3953 0.0398)
  ("sudafricana (Granma, Cu.) ‘a white South-African female doctor’, but Emilia Saura, la" 0.1307 0.8460 0.7246 0.7410 0.7410 0.0165 0.0050 0.3624 0.0912)
  ("médico sin hospital (AM, Mex.) ‘ES, the doctor without a hospital’. El País and the Academy" 0.1307 0.9059 0.7411 0.7575 0.7574 0.0165 0.0050 0.3951 0.0565)
  ("approve of la médica and it is gaining ground though some people still considerate it" 0.1307 0.8527 0.7576 0.7740 0.7739 0.0165 0.0050 0.3215 0.0712)
  ("slightly disrespectful. La doctora is polite alternative for a woman doctor. The Academy" 0.1307 0.8757 0.7741 0.7905 0.7904 0.0165 0.0050 0.3407 0.0646)
  ("rejects la médico." 0.1307 0.2677 0.7906 0.8070 0.8069 0.0165 0.0050 0.3159 0.0538)
  ("• El/la miembro ‘member’ (of clubs), also el socio/la socia. The NGLE 2.9f approves of la" 0.1092 0.8471 0.8071 0.8235 0.8234 0.0165 0.0048 0.3717 0.0627)
  ("miembro." 0.1305 0.2027 0.8237 0.8399 0.8399 0.0163 nil 0.3681 0.0722)
  ("• El/la ministro ‘minister’, but la ministra is usual nowadays. El País and the Academy" 0.1092 0.8481 0.8400 0.8565 0.8564 0.0165 0.0048 0.3776 0.0627)
  ("recommend la primera ministra over la primer ministro ‘prime minister’ although it logically" 0.1305 0.8970 0.8565 0.8730 0.8729 0.0165 0.0048 0.3866 0.1018)
  ("means ‘the first female minister’." 0.1305 0.4102 0.8730 0.8895 0.8895 0.0165 0.0048 0.4060 0.0552)
  ("• La poeta is now preferred to la poetisa ‘poetess’." 0.1092 0.5337 0.8895 0.9060 0.9059 0.0165 0.0048 0.3093 0.0438)
  ("• La política is accepted by the NGLE 2.6g for a female politician; it also means ‘politics’. La" 0.1092 0.8975 0.9060 0.9225 0.9224 0.0165 0.0048 0.3541 0.0438)
  ("informática is a female IT expert; it also means ‘computing’." 0.1305 0.6426 0.9225 0.9390 0.9389 0.0165 0.0048 0.3763 0.1017))
  (18
  ("1.2 Group A: gender of nouns referring to human beings and to a few animals 5" 0.2845 0.9088 0.0492 0.0683 0.0679 0.0154 0.0046 0.3503 0.0228)
  ("• La sacerdotisa is a possible feminine of el sacerdote ‘priest’, mainly used for ancient" 0.1092 0.8339 0.0832 0.0997 0.0995 0.0165 0.0048 0.3248 0.0438)
  ("religions. The NGLE notes the increasing use of la sacerdote for female (i.e. non-Roman" 0.1305 0.8586 0.0997 0.1162 0.1161 0.0165 0.0048 0.3428 0.0787)
  ("Catholic) priests." 0.1305 0.2742 0.1162 0.1327 0.1327 0.0165 0.0048 0.2989 0.0775)
  ("• La sargenta is used to mean a bad-tempered, fierce woman, so la sargento is a female" 0.1092 0.8539 0.1327 0.1492 0.1490 0.0165 0.0050 0.3708 0.0441)
  ("sergeant." 0.1307 0.2080 0.1492 0.1657 0.1657 0.0165 nil 0.1719 0.0773)
  ("Other nouns ending in -o may be regular: el arquitecto/la arquitecta ‘architect’, el biólogo/la bióloga" 0.0920 0.9081 0.1779 0.1943 0.1942 0.0165 0.0058 0.2942 0.0495)
  ("‘biologist’, el catedrático/la catedrática ‘professor’ (European meaning), el filósofo/la filósofa ‘philoso-" 0.0920 0.9081 0.1944 0.2108 0.2107 0.0165 0.0044 0.3159 0.0892)
  ("pher’, el letrado/la letrada ‘counsel’/‘legal representative’, el sociólogo/la socióloga ‘sociologist’, etc." 0.0920 0.9080 0.2109 0.2273 0.2272 0.0165 0.0054 0.2660 0.0512)
  ("Nevertheless, forms like la arquitecto, la filósofo, la letrado may be preferred in Spain. La magistrada" 0.0920 0.9080 0.2274 0.2438 0.2437 0.0165 0.0047 0.3455 0.1156)
  ("‘judge’ (higher in rank than a British magistrate) is now usual." 0.0920 0.6204 0.2439 0.2603 0.2603 0.0165 0.0048 0.3495 0.0585)
  ("(1) Feminine forms are often used, even in educated speech, when the woman is not listening:" 0.0920 0.9081 0.2725 0.2890 0.2888 0.0165 0.0059 0.3212 0.0225)
  ("¿qué tal te llevas con la nueva jefa? ‘how are you getting on with your new woman boss?’, but me" 0.0920 0.9079 0.2890 0.3055 0.3054 0.0165 0.0054 0.3477 0.0367)
  ("han dicho que usted es la jefe del departamento ‘they tell me that you are the head of the department’." 0.0920 0.9081 0.3055 0.3220 0.3219 0.0165 0.0044 0.3470 0.0289)
  ("1.2.8 Nouns referring to mixed groups of males and females" 0.0920 0.7909 0.3461 0.3669 0.3669 0.0208 0.0068 0.3504 0.0541)
  ("With the rare exceptions noted at 1.2.1, the masculine plural refers either to males or to both" 0.0920 0.9080 0.3746 0.3910 0.3910 0.0165 0.0071 0.2918 0.0413)
  ("sexes, which confuses English-speakers. Mis hijos means ‘my sons’ or ‘my children’; mis hermanos" 0.0920 0.9081 0.3911 0.4075 0.4074 0.0165 0.0044 0.3830 0.0495)
  ("means ‘my brothers’ or ‘my brother(s) and sister(s)’. The answer to ¿tienes hermanos? might be" 0.0920 0.9082 0.4075 0.4240 0.4239 0.0165 0.0062 0.3244 0.0552)
  ("tengo dos hermanos y una hermana ‘I’ve got two brothers and one sister’. Likewise hoy vienen los" 0.0920 0.9080 0.4240 0.4405 0.4404 0.0165 0.0062 0.2794 0.0428)
  ("padres de los niños ‘the children’s parents are coming today’. ‘The children’s fathers are coming’" 0.0920 0.9080 0.4405 0.4570 0.4568 0.0165 0.0057 0.2996 0.0499)
  ("would have to be clarified by vienen los padres de los niños—los padres solos = ‘the fathers on their" 0.0920 0.9081 0.4570 0.4735 0.4734 0.0165 0.0053 0.3131 0.0556)
  ("own’. Further examples:" 0.0920 0.2990 0.4735 0.4900 0.4900 0.0165 0.0048 0.3536 0.0480)
  ("los alumnos students/male students" 0.1092 0.4100 0.5043 0.5208 0.5207 0.0165 0.0048 0.3280 0.0214)
  ("los ingleses the English/English men" 0.1092 0.4152 0.5208 0.5373 0.5372 0.0165 0.0048 0.3388 0.0214)
  ("los niños children/little boys" 0.1092 0.3486 0.5373 0.5538 0.5537 0.0165 0.0048 0.3015 0.0214)
  ("los perros dogs/male dogs" 0.1092 0.3283 0.5538 0.5703 0.5702 0.0165 0.0048 0.3182 0.0214)
  ("los primos cousins/male cousins" 0.5233 0.7935 0.5044 0.5208 0.5207 0.0165 0.0048 0.3353 0.0214)
  ("los profesores teachers/male teachers" 0.5233 0.8285 0.5209 0.5373 0.5372 0.0165 0.0048 0.2798 0.0214)
  ("los reyes the King and the Queen/kings/" 0.5233 0.8666 0.5374 0.5538 0.5537 0.0165 0.0048 0.3166 0.0214)
  ("  the kings and queens" 0.5233 0.7230 0.5539 0.5703 0.5701 0.0165 0.0048 0.2580 0.0460)
  ("(1) Feminine nouns refer to females only, so one uses the masculine in sentences like no tengo más" 0.0920 0.9081 0.5847 0.6011 0.6009 0.0165 0.0042 0.3553 0.0225)
  ("amigos que mujeres ‘the only friends I have are women’ or todos los profesores son mujeres ‘all the" 0.0920 0.9081 0.6012 0.6176 0.6175 0.0165 0.0058 0.3114 0.0578)
  ("teachers are women’. ?No tengo más amigas que mujeres means ‘the only women friends I have are" 0.0920 0.9079 0.6177 0.6341 0.6340 0.0165 0.0047 0.3703 0.0699)
  ("women’! Tú eres la más inteligente de todos ‘you’re the most intelligent of all’ is a better compliment" 0.0920 0.9081 0.6341 0.6506 0.6505 0.0165 0.0040 0.4066 0.0747)
  ("to a woman than . . .de todas since the feminine excludes males. But a sentence like María es la" 0.0920 0.9080 0.6506 0.6671 0.6670 0.0165 0.0063 0.3386 0.0168)
  ("mejor profesora del instituto ‘Maria’s the best teacher in the school’ is ambiguous: it may or may not" 0.0920 0.9080 0.6671 0.6836 0.6835 0.0165 0.0041 0.3895 0.0439)
  ("include males. Emilia Pardo Bazán es la mejor intérprete de la vida rural de toda la literatura española" 0.0920 0.9080 0.6836 0.7001 0.7000 0.0163 0.0056 0.2965 0.0636)
  ("del siglo XIX ‘Emilia Pardo Bazán is the best interpreter of rural life in the whole of nineteenth-" 0.0920 0.9080 0.7001 0.7166 0.7165 0.0165 0.0058 0.3175 0.0225)
  ("century Spanish literature’ is assumed to mean that she is better than everybody. If ‘the best" 0.0920 0.9080 0.7166 0.7331 0.7331 0.0165 0.0073 0.2610 0.0652)
  ("female interpreter’ were meant one would say intérprete femenina." 0.0920 0.6467 0.7331 0.7496 0.7495 0.0165 0.0048 0.3573 0.0571)
  ("(2) Care must be taken with words like uno, otro. If a woman from Madrid says todos los madrile-" 0.0920 0.9081 0.7618 0.7783 0.7781 0.0165 0.0049 0.3882 0.0225)
  ("ños me caen gordos ‘all Madrid people get on my nerves’ one could reply ¡pero tú eres uno de ellos!" 0.0920 0.9081 0.7783 0.7948 0.7947 0.0165 0.0049 0.3332 0.0268)
  ("‘but you’re one of them!’, but not * . . . una de ellos, since madrileños includes both males and" 0.0920 0.9080 0.7948 0.8113 0.8112 0.0165 0.0074 0.3024 0.0339)
  ("females (¡pero tú también eres madrileña! avoids the problem). Compare also Ana es una de las" 0.0920 0.9081 0.8113 0.8278 0.8277 0.0165 0.0076 0.2932 0.0653)
  ("profesoras ‘Ana is one of the women teachers’ and Ana es uno de los profesores ‘Ana is one of the" 0.0920 0.9080 0.8278 0.8443 0.8442 0.0165 0.0056 0.3095 0.0810)
  ("teachers’. In a few cases, usage seems uncertain. A woman might say either unos están a favor y" 0.0920 0.9080 0.8443 0.8608 0.8607 0.0165 0.0059 0.3288 0.0801)
  ("otros en contra. Yo soy de las que están a favor or . . . de los que están a favor ‘some are for, others are" 0.0920 0.9080 0.8608 0.8773 0.8772 0.0165 0.0055 0.2729 0.0382)
  ("against. I’m one of those who are for it’." 0.0920 0.4292 0.8773 0.8938 0.8938 0.0165 0.0048 0.3716 0.0660)
  ("(3) The fact that the masculine includes the feminine irritates some feminists since a phrase like" 0.0920 0.9081 0.9060 0.9225 0.9223 0.0165 0.0050 0.3417 0.0225)
  ("oportunidades para alumnos de química ‘opportunities for students of chemistry’ does not clearly" 0.0920 0.9080 0.9225 0.9390 0.9389 0.0165 0.0064 0.2752 0.1102))
  (19
  ("6 Gender of nouns" 0.0905 0.2404 0.0492 0.0683 0.0679 0.0154 0.0046 0.3029 0.0124)
  ("include females, so in notices and pamphlets one sometimes sees alumn@s, candidat@s, etc. L@s" 0.0920 0.9080 0.0832 0.0997 0.0996 0.0165 0.0058 0.3413 0.0636)
  ("alumn@s is a gender-neutral (and unpronounceable) way of writing los alumnos y las alumnas" 0.0919 0.9080 0.0997 0.1162 0.1161 0.0165 0.0075 0.2783 0.0722)
  ("‘male and female students’. The Academy disapproves of this use of @." 0.0919 0.6954 0.1162 0.1327 0.1327 0.0165 0.0048 0.3629 0.0468)
  ("1.2.9 Gender of nouns denoting non-living things when they are" 0.0920 0.8382 0.1568 0.1776 0.1776 0.0208 0.0068 0.3259 0.1515)
  ("applied to humans" 0.1582 0.3745 0.1769 0.1976 0.1976 0.0208 0.0068 0.3347 0.0852)
  ("Feminine nouns that usually apply to lifeless things can sometimes be applied to human males." 0.0920 0.9081 0.2053 0.2218 0.2218 0.0165 0.0052 0.3474 0.0798)
